+## History: R1 (Ruby 3.0 -> 3.2, still Rails 6.1)
+
+### Ruby 3.2
+
+* `.ruby-version` is `3.2.9`. `Gemfile.lock` was re-resolved on Ruby 3.2:
+  `nokogiri` is 1.18.9 (the `Gemfile` already selects it for Ruby >= 3.1),
+  plus platform-specific `ffi` and `nokogiri` builds.
+* Ruby 3.0 and 3.2 use the same Marshal format, so session cookies are
+  readable both ways (verified against R0 running on Ruby 3.0).
+* CI runs Ruby 3.2 only, with both Gemfiles.
+
+### Rollback R1 -> R0
+
+R1 used the same Rails version and framework defaults as R0, so it wrote
+byte-for-byte what R0 writes. The code added in R1 only made R1 read more.
+
+### Making R1 a safe rollback target for R2 (Rails 7.0 defaults)
+
+| Rails 7.0 default (via `load_defaults 7.0`) | What R2 writes | How R1 reads it |
+|---|---|---|
+| `active_support.key_generator_hash_digest_class = SHA256` | every derived key changes: session cookie keys, `message_verifier(:mail_links)` | **`Redmine::KeyRotation`** registers the *other* PBKDF2 digest as a read-only fallback: cookie rotations for both ciphers, plus a rotation on every `Rails.application.message_verifier`. The native digest is detected from the app's real key generator. |
+| `action_dispatch.use_authenticated_cookie_encryption` (5.2) | AES-256-GCM cookies instead of AES-256-CBC+HMAC | Rails 6.1 already reads GCM with the native key derivation. `KeyRotation` adds GCM and CBC with the other derivation. |
+| `action_dispatch.cookies_serializer = :json` | JSON session data | Rails 6.1 `:marshal` falls back to JSON on read. JSON turns nested symbol keys into strings, so session readers accept both shapes: `QueriesHelper#normalize_query_session_data` and `AccountController#register` (`auth_source_registration`). |
+| `action_dispatch.use_cookies_with_metadata` (6.0) | purpose/expiry embedded in cookies | Rails 6.1 reads it natively. |
+| `action_controller.per_form_csrf_tokens` (5.0) | per-form CSRF tokens in every form | R1's `ApplicationController#valid_per_form_csrf_token?` always accepts a valid per-form token (removed in R2, which accepts them natively). |
+| `action_controller.urlsafe_csrf_tokens` (6.1) | URL-safe base64 CSRF tokens | Rails 6.1 decoding falls back to URL-safe. |
+| `active_support.cache_format_version = 7.0` | 7.0 cache entries | Rails 6.1 treats them as misses and rewrites them. |
+| `active_support.hash_digest_class = SHA256` | different ETags and fragment-cache keys | Cache misses and 200 instead of 304, once. Harmless. |
+| `action_controller.forgery_protection_origin_check` (5.0) | R2 *rejects* POSTs whose `Origin` doesn't match the base URL, which signs the user out | Not a rollback issue but a sign-out risk for R2's deploy. R1 logs `... would fail forgery_protection_origin_check` (see the R2 pre-deploy checklist). |
+
+Other Rails 7.0 app fixes made in R1 (they apply to both versions):
+`belongs_to_required_by_default = false` (permanent); `redirect_to_referer_or`
+passes `allow_other_host: true`; `AttachmentsController#upload` checks
+`request.media_type`; `to_s(:db)` became `to_formatted_s(:db)`; the
+`*.api.rsb` lookup uses a `LookupContext#formats=` hook, and the API builders
+set the response content type.
diff --git a/extra/upgrade_check/check.rb b/extra/upgrade_check/check.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..f0b3b78
--- /dev/null
+++ b/extra/upgrade_check/check.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,99 @@
+# frozen_string_literal: true
+
+# Cross-release check, step 2: run with the release that reads (the next
+# release for an upgrade, the previous one for a rollback).
+#   RAILS_ENV=test bin/rails db:fixtures:load
+#   RAILS_ENV=test bin/rails runner extra/upgrade_check/check.rb /tmp/artifacts.json
+# Prints OK, or FAIL with what broke (sign-out, CSRF, mail link, query state,
+# cache).
+# See docs/UPGRADE.md.
+require 'json'
+require 'action_dispatch/testing/integration'
+ActionController::Base.allow_forgery_protection = true # off in the test env
+art = JSON.parse(File.read(ARGV[0]))
+app = Rails.application
+fails = []
+label = "minted by Rails #{art['rails']} (Ruby #{art['ruby'] || '?'}), checked on Rails #{Rails.version} (Ruby #{RUBY_VERSION})"
+
+if art['secret_fingerprint'] && art['secret_fingerprint'] != Digest::SHA256.hexdigest(app.secret_key_base)
+  fails << 'different secret_key_base: everybody would be signed out and every mail link broken'
+end
+
+begin
+  data = Mailer.verify_mail_link(art['mail_token'], :unwatch)
+  fails << "mail link: wrong payload #{data.inspect}" unless data == {'user_id' => 3, 'issue_id' => 2}
+rescue => e
+  fails << "mail link rejected (#{e.class})"
+end
+
+s = ActionDispatch::Integration::Session.new(app)
+s.host! 'www.example.com'
+s.cookies['_redmine_session'] = art['session_cookie']
+s.get '/my/account'
+if s.response.status != 200
+  fails << "session cookie not accepted: GET /my/account -> #{s.response.status} (signed out)"
+else
+  # submit the form that was rendered by the other release
+  s.put '/my/account', params: {authenticity_token: art['form_token'], user: {firstname: 'John'}}
+  fails << "form token rejected: PUT /my/account -> #{s.response.status}" unless s.response.status == 302
+  s.get '/my/account'
+  fails << "signed out after form post (#{s.response.status})" unless s.response.status == 200
+end
+
+s2 = ActionDispatch::Integration::Session.new(app)
+s2.host! 'www.example.com'
+s2.cookies['_redmine_session'] = art['session_cookie_with_query']
+s2.get '/projects/ecookbook/issues'
+if s2.response.status != 200
+  fails << "issues list with saved query -> #{s2.response.status}"
+else
+  # the stored filter was "closed issues" sorted by id desc; issue 8 is closed.
+  ids = s2.response.body.scan(/<tr id="issue-(\d+)"/).flatten.map(&:to_i)
+  fails << "saved query lost: issue ids #{ids.inspect}" unless ids.any? && ids.include?(8) && ids == ids.sort.reverse
+end
+
+# A saved query written by the other release, in the shared database
+if art['query_id']
+  begin
+    query = IssueQuery.find(art['query_id'])
+    expected = [{'status_id' => {operator: 'c', values: ['']}, 'tracker_id' => {operator: '=', values: %w(1 2 3)}},
+                [:tracker, :status, :subject], [%w(id desc)], 'tracker', [:estimated_hours]]
+    actual = [query.filters, query.column_names, query.sort_criteria.to_a, query.group_by, query.totalable_names]
+    fails << "saved query: #{actual.inspect}" unless actual == expected
+    s3 = ActionDispatch::Integration::Session.new(app)
+    s3.host! 'www.example.com'
+    s3.cookies['_redmine_session'] = art['session_cookie']
+    s3.get '/projects/ecookbook/issues', params: {query_id: query.id}
+    ids = s3.response.body.scan(/<tr id="issue-(\d+)"/).flatten.map(&:to_i)
+    fails << "saved query: GET issues -> #{s3.response.status} #{ids.inspect}" unless s3.response.status == 200 && ids.include?(8)
+  rescue ActiveRecord::RecordNotFound
+    fails << 'saved query not found: run both sides with the same DATABASE_URL'
+  rescue => e
+    fails << "saved query raised #{e.class}: #{e.message.lines.first.strip}"
+  end
+end
+
+# A cache entry written by the other release must be read or be a miss. It
+# must never raise: Redmine reads the cache on almost every page.
+if art['cache_dir']
+  begin
+    value = ActiveSupport::Cache.lookup_store(:file_store, art['cache_dir']).read('upgrade_check')
+    if value.nil?
+      puts "note: the cache entry is a miss (it will be rewritten)"
+    elsif value != 'formatted text ' * 300
+      fails << "cache entry: wrong value"
+    end
+  rescue => e
+    fails << "cache read raised #{e.class}: #{e.message.lines.first.strip}"
+  end
+end
+
+exit_code = fails.empty? ? 0 : 1
+if fails.empty?
+  puts "OK   #{label}"
+else
+  puts "FAIL #{label}"; fails.each { |f| puts "  - #{f}" }
+end
+# Exit once the runner is done: Rails 8.1's runner reports a SystemExit
+# raised by the script as an error.
+at_exit { exit exit_code }
diff --git a/extra/upgrade_check/mint.rb b/extra/upgrade_check/mint.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..cc5e90a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/extra/upgrade_check/mint.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,62 @@
+# frozen_string_literal: true
+
+# Cross-release check, step 1: run with the release that writes.
+#   RAILS_ENV=test bin/rails db:fixtures:load
+#   RAILS_ENV=test bin/rails runner extra/upgrade_check/mint.rb /tmp/artifacts.json
+# Mints a session cookie (with saved query state), a form CSRF token and an
+# unwatch link exactly as a running release does, and a cache entry in a file
+# store shared with the reader (UPGRADE_CHECK_CACHE, default
+# tmp/upgrade_check_cache). The scripts don't depend on the Rails version:
+# run the same copy in the trees of both releases. See docs/UPGRADE.md.
+require 'json'
+require 'action_dispatch/testing/integration'
+ActionController::Base.allow_forgery_protection = true # off in the test env
+app = Rails.application
+out = {'rails' => Rails.version, 'ruby' => RUBY_VERSION}
+# A different secret_key_base signs everybody out: compare fingerprints
+out['secret_fingerprint'] = Digest::SHA256.hexdigest(app.secret_key_base)
+
+# 1) mail link (as sent in a notification mail)
+out['mail_token'] = Mailer.mail_link_token(:unwatch, 'user_id' => 3, 'issue_id' => 2)
+
+# 2) real session cookie + per-form CSRF token, through the full middleware stack
+s = ActionDispatch::Integration::Session.new(app)
+s.host! 'www.example.com'
+user = User.find(2) # jsmith
+s.get '/login'
+s.post '/login', params: {username: 'jsmith', password: 'jsmith', authenticity_token: Nokogiri::HTML(s.response.body).at_css('input[name=authenticity_token]')['value']}
+raise "login failed: #{s.response.status}" unless s.response.status == 302
+
+s.get '/my/account'
+raise "my/account: #{s.response.status}" unless s.response.status == 200
+
+out['session_cookie'] = s.cookies['_redmine_session']
+form_token = Nokogiri::HTML(s.response.body).at_css("form#my_account_form input[name=authenticity_token]")["value"]
+out['form_token'] = form_token
+# Saved issue query state in the session
+s.get '/projects/ecookbook/issues', params: {set_filter: 1, f: ['status_id'], op: {'status_id' => 'c'}, sort: 'id:desc'}
+out['session_cookie_with_query'] = s.cookies['_redmine_session']
+# A saved query: a database row with YAML-serialized columns (filters,
+# columns, sort, options), written through the UI. The reader must see the
+# same database (in production both releases share it): run both sides
+# with the same DATABASE_URL.
+s.get '/projects/ecookbook/queries/new'
+query_name = "upgrade check #{SecureRandom.hex(4)}"
+s.post '/projects/ecookbook/queries', params: {
+  authenticity_token: Nokogiri::HTML(s.response.body).at_css('form#query-form input[name=authenticity_token]')['value'],
+  query: {name: query_name, visibility: '0', group_by: 'tracker'},
+  f: %w(status_id tracker_id), op: {'status_id' => 'c', 'tracker_id' => '='}, v: {'tracker_id' => %w(1 2 3)},
+  c: %w(tracker status subject), t: %w(estimated_hours), sort: 'id:desc'
+}
+raise "saving a query failed: #{s.response.status}" unless s.response.status == 302
+
+out['query_id'] = IssueQuery.find_by!(name: query_name).id
+# 3) cache entry, in the cache format of the running release (like the
+#    formatted wiki text and language options that Redmine caches)
+cache_dir = ENV['UPGRADE_CHECK_CACHE'] || File.expand_path('../../tmp/upgrade_check_cache', __dir__)
+FileUtils.rm_rf(cache_dir)
+ActiveSupport::Cache.lookup_store(:file_store, cache_dir).write('upgrade_check', 'formatted text ' * 300)
+out['cache_dir'] = cache_dir
+
+File.write(ARGV[0], JSON.pretty_generate(out))
+puts "minted with Rails #{Rails.version}"
diff --git a/extra/upgrade_check/rails61_leftovers.rb b/extra/upgrade_check/rails61_leftovers.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5e59efb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/extra/upgrade_check/rails61_leftovers.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@
+# frozen_string_literal: true
+
+# Deploy gate for R4 (Rails 8.1), see docs/UPGRADE.md. R4 no longer reads
+# what the Rails 6.1 release (R1) wrote: Marshal session cookies and keys
+# derived with PBKDF2-SHA1 (session cookies, unwatch links in mails).
+#
+# Run it in production, on the deployed release, before deploying R4:
+#   RAILS_ENV=production bin/rails runner extra/upgrade_check/rails61_leftovers.rb "<time R2 was deployed>"
+#
+# * Unwatch links sent by R1 are valid for 30 days after they were sent, so
+#   at most until 30 days after R2 replaced R1.
+# * An R1 session cookie is rewritten in the current format on the first
+#   request after R2 was deployed (and that request marks its session token
+#   as used). So an R1 cookie can only still sign someone in if its session
+#   token is still valid and wasn't used since R2 was deployed.
+#
+# Exits with 0 if no R1 artifact can still be live, 1 otherwise.
+abort 'usage: rails61_leftovers.rb "<time R2 was deployed, eg. 2026-01-31 08:00 UTC>"' if ARGV[0].blank?
+r2_deployed_at = Time.zone.parse(ARGV[0]) || abort("invalid time: #{ARGV[0]}")
+blockers = []
+
+mail_links_expire_at = r2_deployed_at + 30.days
+if mail_links_expire_at > Time.now
+  blockers << "unwatch links sent by R1 are valid until #{mail_links_expire_at}"
+end
+
+sessions = Token.where(:action => 'session').where('updated_on < ?', r2_deployed_at)
+if Setting.session_lifetime?
+  sessions = sessions.where('created_on > ?', Setting.session_lifetime.to_i.minutes.ago)
+end
+if Setting.session_timeout?
+  sessions = sessions.where('updated_on > ?', Setting.session_timeout.to_i.minutes.ago)
+end
+users = User.active.where(:id => sessions.select(:user_id))
+if users.any?
+  blockers << "#{sessions.where(:user_id => users.select(:id)).count} session(s) of #{users.count} active " \
+              "user(s) not used since R2 was deployed can still be valid (R1 cookies)"
+end
+
+if blockers.empty?
+  puts 'OK   no artifact written by R1 can still be live: R4 can be deployed'
+else
+  puts 'WAIT R4 would sign out or break mail links:'
+  blockers.each {|b| puts "  - #{b}"}
+end
+at_exit { exit(blockers.empty? ? 0 : 1) }
